WEBVTT

00:00:00.550 --> 00:00:02.135
Lesson 2.1.

00:00:02.135 --> 00:00:04.290
DEXs, aggregators & routing.

00:00:04.290 --> 00:00:06.322
By the end, you'll be able to choose

00:00:06.322 --> 00:00:08.628
where to swap by comparing the net amount

00:00:08.628 --> 00:00:10.989
you actually receive, not the quoted price.

00:00:12.289 --> 00:00:13.579
Here's the plan.

00:00:13.579 --> 00:00:16.021
First, the three places a swap can actually

00:00:16.021 --> 00:00:18.984
happen, and what an aggregator does differently.

00:00:18.984 --> 00:00:22.693
Second, a real worked example showing exactly when routing

00:00:22.693 --> 00:00:25.444
wins, and when it quietly loses to gas.

00:00:25.444 --> 00:00:27.223
And third, a checklist to run

00:00:27.223 --> 00:00:29.239
before you click swap, every time.

00:00:30.539 --> 00:00:31.870
Every swap you make shows

00:00:31.870 --> 00:00:33.449
you a quoted price first.

00:00:33.449 --> 00:00:36.024
That number is not what lands in your wallet.

00:00:36.024 --> 00:00:39.220
What you actually get equals the quoted output,

00:00:39.220 --> 00:00:42.649
minus price impact, minus pool fees, minus gas.

00:00:42.649 --> 00:00:44.418
Chase the quote, and you can end

00:00:44.418 --> 00:00:46.240
up worse off than the option that

00:00:46.240 --> 00:00:48.009
looked less impressive on screen.

00:00:49.309 --> 00:00:50.514
Three ways to swap.

00:00:51.814 --> 00:00:54.354
So, three venues, side by side.

00:00:54.354 --> 00:00:57.967
An A.M.M. deck, an automated market maker, means you

00:00:57.967 --> 00:01:01.581
trade against a liquidity pool, priced by a formula,

00:01:01.581 --> 00:01:04.744
which lesson two point two covers in full.

00:01:04.744 --> 00:01:07.225
An on-chain order book means your order

00:01:07.225 --> 00:01:09.520
is matched by price and time against

00:01:09.520 --> 00:01:12.239
other orders, fully or partly on-chain.

00:01:12.239 --> 00:01:15.031
And an aggregator doesn't hold liquidity itself,

00:01:15.031 --> 00:01:17.139
it searches many pools and venues at

00:01:17.139 --> 00:01:19.304
once, and may split your order across

00:01:19.304 --> 00:01:21.469
several routes to get a better result.

00:01:22.769 --> 00:01:25.964
Here's what an aggregator actually does with your order.

00:01:25.964 --> 00:01:27.629
You submit one swap.

00:01:27.629 --> 00:01:30.190
The aggregator scans prices across every

00:01:30.190 --> 00:01:32.364
pool and venue it knows about.

00:01:32.364 --> 00:01:35.559
Rather than sending your whole order to one pool,

00:01:35.559 --> 00:01:37.988
it can split it: part to pool A, part

00:01:37.988 --> 00:01:39.949
to pool B, part to pool C.

00:01:39.949 --> 00:01:42.236
Each one absorbs less of the trade, so

00:01:42.236 --> 00:01:44.529
each one moves its own price less.

00:01:44.529 --> 00:01:46.734
Those three fills recombine into one

00:01:46.734 --> 00:01:48.104
net output back to you.

00:01:49.404 --> 00:01:50.494
Quick check.

00:01:50.494 --> 00:01:53.147
Why can an aggregator often give a better price

00:01:53.147 --> 00:01:55.469
than sending your whole order to one pool?

00:01:59.889 --> 00:02:02.052
The answer: it can split an order

00:02:02.052 --> 00:02:04.787
across several pools, so each pool absorbs

00:02:04.787 --> 00:02:07.204
less, and each one's price moves less.

00:02:08.504 --> 00:02:09.689
What moves the number.

00:02:10.989 --> 00:02:14.089
Two things decide whether splitting actually helps.

00:02:14.089 --> 00:02:16.391
Price impact is how much your own trade

00:02:16.391 --> 00:02:18.693
moves the price, and it grows with your

00:02:18.693 --> 00:02:20.949
size relative to the pool's depth.

00:02:20.949 --> 00:02:24.049
Slippage tolerance is the worst price you'll accept

00:02:24.049 --> 00:02:27.509
before your transaction reverts instead of executing.

00:02:27.509 --> 00:02:30.186
And routing itself just means the path your

00:02:30.186 --> 00:02:32.924
order takes, sometimes multiple hops, A to B

00:02:32.924 --> 00:02:35.175
to C, which can reach a better price

00:02:35.175 --> 00:02:37.669
but touches more contracts along the way.

00:02:38.969 --> 00:02:41.864
Here's the pattern that actually decides the winner.

00:02:41.864 --> 00:02:44.174
On a small trade, your price impact

00:02:44.174 --> 00:02:46.355
is already tiny at a single pool,

00:02:46.355 --> 00:02:48.922
so an aggregator's improvement is a few

00:02:48.922 --> 00:02:51.724
dollars, easily eaten by its extra gas.

00:02:51.724 --> 00:02:53.914
On a large trade, your price impact

00:02:53.914 --> 00:02:56.099
at a single pool is real money.

00:02:56.099 --> 00:02:59.113
Splitting it across several pools, each moving

00:02:59.113 --> 00:03:01.999
less, saves real money too, comfortably past

00:03:01.999 --> 00:03:03.474
the extra gas it costs.

00:03:04.774 --> 00:03:05.864
Quick check.

00:03:05.864 --> 00:03:07.829
What actually drives price impact?

00:03:12.249 --> 00:03:14.518
The answer: your trade size, relative

00:03:14.518 --> 00:03:16.309
to the pool's liquidity depth.

00:03:17.609 --> 00:03:19.764
Big trades benefit from routing.

00:03:19.764 --> 00:03:22.054
Small trades mostly lose to gas.

00:03:22.054 --> 00:03:24.053
That single line is the whole lesson,

00:03:24.053 --> 00:03:25.999
if you remember nothing else from it.

00:03:27.299 --> 00:03:28.364
A worked example.

00:03:29.664 --> 00:03:36.253
Selling 20 ETH. A single pool, direct, quotes 59,100

00:03:36.253 --> 00:03:42.024
USDC, costs about $8 in gas, netting 59,092.

00:03:42.024 --> 00:03:48.088
An aggregator, splitting across three pools, quotes 59,420,

00:03:48.088 --> 00:03:52.329
costs about $20 in gas, netting 59,400.

00:03:52.329 --> 00:03:55.949
The aggregator wins here by about $308.

00:03:57.249 --> 00:04:00.440
Now the same comparison at a much smaller size:

00:04:00.440 --> 00:04:04.229
selling zero point five E.T.H. The aggregator's quote is

00:04:04.229 --> 00:04:07.454
three dollars better than the direct pool's.

00:04:07.454 --> 00:04:10.129
But its route costs twelve dollars more in gas.

00:04:10.129 --> 00:04:13.144
Net, the aggregator is nine dollars worse off.

00:04:13.144 --> 00:04:15.399
At this size, the direct pool wins.

00:04:16.699 --> 00:04:18.424
Last check for this section.

00:04:18.424 --> 00:04:20.849
When does the best-looking quote actually lose?

00:04:25.269 --> 00:04:27.725
The answer: when the extra gas its route

00:04:27.725 --> 00:04:30.361
costs is more than the price improvement it

00:04:30.361 --> 00:04:32.757
offers, which is common on small trades.

00:04:34.057 --> 00:04:35.122
Where this goes wrong.

00:04:36.422 --> 00:04:38.706
Here's how people actually lose money on

00:04:38.706 --> 00:04:41.067
this, even once they know the theory.

00:04:41.067 --> 00:04:44.052
Comparing quoted prices instead of net output, so

00:04:44.052 --> 00:04:46.502
a worse deal looks better on screen.

00:04:46.502 --> 00:04:49.145
Assuming a route through more contracts is

00:04:49.145 --> 00:04:53.201
automatically riskier, when a well-established aggregator routing

00:04:53.201 --> 00:04:56.082
through audited major pools is often fine.

00:04:56.082 --> 00:04:59.012
Setting slippage tolerance far too high just to

00:04:59.012 --> 00:05:01.880
make a transaction go through, which opens the

00:05:01.880 --> 00:05:03.767
door to a much worse fill.

00:05:03.767 --> 00:05:06.240
And routing a small trade through several hops

00:05:06.240 --> 00:05:08.397
anyway, out of habit, when a single pool

00:05:08.397 --> 00:05:09.817
would have kept more of it.

00:05:11.117 --> 00:05:12.312
Before you click swap.

00:05:13.612 --> 00:05:15.295
Run this before every swap,

00:05:15.295 --> 00:05:17.037
not just the large ones.

00:05:17.037 --> 00:05:18.916
Compare net output after gas,

00:05:18.916 --> 00:05:21.220
never the headline quoted price.

00:05:21.220 --> 00:05:23.205
Check that the price impact shown

00:05:23.205 --> 00:05:25.140
is acceptable for this size.

00:05:25.140 --> 00:05:27.600
Confirm the route uses reputable pools

00:05:27.600 --> 00:05:30.170
and tokens you've actually verified.

00:05:30.170 --> 00:05:33.034
And set your slippage tolerance on purpose, which

00:05:33.034 --> 00:05:35.210
lesson two point five covers in depth.

00:05:36.510 --> 00:05:38.725
Your turn, 5 to 10 minutes.

00:05:38.725 --> 00:05:41.180
Pull up a swap you're actually considering.

00:05:41.180 --> 00:05:42.921
Get a direct-pool quote and an

00:05:42.921 --> 00:05:45.187
aggregator quote for the same size.

00:05:45.187 --> 00:05:47.798
Work out the net output for each, quote

00:05:47.798 --> 00:05:50.709
minus price impact minus fees minus gas.

00:05:50.709 --> 00:05:53.279
Then pick whichever number is actually bigger,

00:05:53.279 --> 00:05:55.684
not whichever quote looked better on screen.

00:05:56.983 --> 00:06:00.053
To recap: an AMM prices against a pool, an

00:06:00.053 --> 00:06:03.980
order book matches orders, and an aggregator can split

00:06:03.980 --> 00:06:07.208
across both to chase a better net result.

00:06:07.208 --> 00:06:10.053
Price impact and slippage tolerance are the two

00:06:10.053 --> 00:06:13.258
forces that decide whether splitting is worth it.

00:06:13.258 --> 00:06:16.344
And the rule of thumb holds: big trades benefit

00:06:16.344 --> 00:06:19.302
from routing, small trades mostly lose to gas.

00:06:20.602 --> 00:06:22.997
That's DEXs, aggregators and routing.

00:06:22.997 --> 00:06:27.021
Next up, Lesson 2.2: AMM mathematics, the

00:06:27.021 --> 00:06:30.854
formula behind every pool you just saw,

00:06:30.854 --> 00:06:31.812
x · y = k.
